{
  "_source": "UEV-A2 (docs/eras/uev.md, machines 2-4, lines 3-6): the heavy ion cyclotron, the recoil separator and the superheavy chemistry lab, and the chemistry round them. Real processes: JINR Dubna's U-400 cyclotron fires a Ca-48 beam at actinide targets (Pu-244 -> flerovium, Cm-248 -> livermorium, Cf-249 -> oganesson, Am-243 -> moscovium, a Bk-249 target wheel -> tennessine), a gas-filled recoil separator (DGFRS) filters the recoiling atoms with helium and magnets, and a gas-phase chemistry lab (COMPACT, Fl/Cn volatility on gold) measures them; polycarbonate foil irradiated with heavy ions and etched in NaOH gives track-etched membranes.",
  "_how_it_is_used": [
    "Read by server_scripts/grindless/uev/superheavy.js at every server load. Each entry becomes one recipe of its type, id grindless:<type>/<name>. The three UEV types carry the data key of the type = <name> (their EMI line); the programmed circuit picks the recipe (circuits 1-6, unique inside a type). gtceu:assembler and gtceu:chemical_reactor rows take circuits 20 and up so GTM's own recipes cannot hide a row.",
    "kept[] are non-consumed items (the SRF cavity), charge[] non-consumed fluids (the helium chamber): they survive a run. Every recoil gas goes to the separator, every atom item to the lab; tennessine is stored in a sealed capsule and no UEV recipe takes it. The activated target wheel decays in IV's hot cell (hot_cell.json, target_wheel_return) and comes back as a blank wheel.",
    "The spent sodium hydroxide of the etch is neutralised with hydrochloric acid to salt (GTM's electrolysis takes it); phosgene and the chlorine round it stay in the reactor chain."
  ],
